[[@space-weird-diamonds|Diamonds are pure carbon]], squeezed into crystals about 150–200 km down, in the [[mantle|mantle]]. The heat and pressure there are enormous. Most diamonds are between one and three and a half billion years old.

They reach us thanks to rare, fast volcanic eruptions from very deep down. These blasted up narrow pipes of rock called kimberlite, carrying diamonds with them. Many pipes were later worn down, and rivers washed the diamonds into gravel beds. That is where most early diamonds were found.

{{story:snowflake-six}}

Water flows downhill. On a flat Earth, "downhill" means towards the middle. So the oceans would gather into a giant dome of water over the centre, deeper there than any real sea.

Air would do the same. The atmosphere would bunch up near the middle and thin out towards the edges. People living near the rim would struggle to breathe, like climbers on [[@mountains-tallest|Mount Everest]], and then not be able to breathe at all.

{{story:aryabhata}}

Once a year, many corals on the reef release eggs and sperm on the same night. It usually happens a few nights after a full moon in late spring, which in Australia means November or December. Scientists call this mass spawning. The water fills with tiny pink and white bundles rising like an upside-down snowstorm.

By spawning together, the corals give their eggs the best chance of meeting sperm from other colonies. The young corals drift for days before settling on rock to start new colonies. Divers come from around the world to see it.

{{story:climate-change}}

Not all melting ice raises the sea. Arctic sea ice is already floating, so when it melts, it hardly changes the level, like an ice cube in a glass of water. Ice on land is different. When a glacier in the Himalayas or Alaska melts, its water flows into rivers and then the sea, adding new water.

The largest stores of land ice are in Greenland and [[@antarctica|Antarctica]]. The Antarctic ice sheet is, on average, about 2 km thick. If all of it melted, which would take many centuries, the sea would rise by about 58 m. Nobody expects that soon. But even a small share of that ice slipping into the sea would make a big difference to coasts.

{{story:glaciers}}

The glass tube usually holds hydrogen peroxide, the same chemical used to bleach hair, but weaker. Outside the tube is a liquid called a phenyl oxalate ester, mixed with a dye. Neither liquid glows on its own. You could keep a glow stick in a drawer for years and it would stay dark.

Snap it, and the peroxide meets the ester. They react to form a short-lived, high-energy molecule. Within a split second, that molecule breaks apart and hands its energy to the dye. The dye glows with its own colour. Swap the dye, and you change the light from green to pink to blue. Shaking the stick only mixes the liquids better so the whole tube lights up evenly. The dye decides the colour. Green and yellow-green look especially bright to human eyes, because our eyes are most sensitive to those colours. That is one reason so many safety glow sticks are green.

{{story:bioluminescence}}

Laser speed guns, also called lidar, work a little like a bat's [[@echoes|echoes]]. The gun fires short pulses of invisible infrared [[@lasers|laser]] light at a car and times how long each one takes to bounce back. Light travels about 30 cm in a billionth of a second, so the gun needs an extremely precise clock.

If each pulse takes a tiny bit less time to return than the last, the car is coming closer. The gun fires many pulses in a fraction of a second and works out how fast the distance is shrinking. Because the beam is narrow, an officer can aim it at one vehicle in heavy traffic.

{{story:road-safety}}

A bhunga is a round house with thick mud walls and a cone-shaped thatched roof. It stays cool in summer and warm in winter, but the windows are small, so the inside can be dim. The mirrors bounce the light from the doorway or from an oil lamp around the room. At night, one flame makes the walls glitter like stars.

Bhungas also proved surprisingly tough in a disaster. When a huge earthquake struck Kutch in 2001, many round mud houses survived while square concrete buildings nearby collapsed. Their circular shape spreads the shaking evenly, a lesson engineers still study when they look at [[@earthquakes|earthquakes]].

{{story:earthquakes}}

Frida had planned to study medicine. Now, stuck in bed, she began to paint. Her mother had a special easel made that let her paint lying down, and a [[@mirrors|mirror]] was fixed above the bed so she could see herself.

That was the start of her self-portraits. Around 55 of her paintings show her own face. She painted herself with her pets, in hospital, with her heart outside her body, or as two Fridas holding hands. Each picture told a piece of her story.

{{story:polio-india}}

Here is a puzzle the Greek writer Plutarch described almost 2,000 years ago. The hero Theseus had a wooden ship. Over the years, as planks rotted, workers replaced them one at a time. In the end, not one original plank was left. Is it still the ship of Theseus?

Now suppose someone collected all the old planks and built a second ship from them. Which one is the real ship? There is no measuring tape that can settle this. You have to think about what "the same" means, and that is philosophy. It matters too, because your own body replaces many of its cells, yet you feel like the same person.

{{story:paradoxes}}

The final step is rendering. The computer works out the colour of every [[@pixels|pixel]] in every frame, based on the shapes, the materials and where the lights are. A single frame can take hours.

{{story:pixels}}

To get it done, Pixar uses a "render farm" of thousands of computers working together. Pixar also wrote its own software, called RenderMan, which many other studios use for visual effects.

Versions of chasing games are very old in India. In the early 1900s, the Deccan Gymkhana club in Pune wrote down the first set of kho-kho rules, around 1914, and the court took its modern shape over the next few decades.

In 1936, an Indian sports club from Amravati travelled to the Berlin Olympics and gave demonstrations of Indian sports, including kho-kho and [[@sport-mallakhamb|mallakhamb]]. It wasn't an official event, but European crowds saw kho-kho for the first time.

{{story:sport-indian-games}}

For most of the sport's history, jumpers landed in pits of sand or sawdust. Coming down head first would have been dangerous. In the 1960s, many schools in the USA switched to thick foam landing mats. Fosbury could now land on his back safely, and his style became possible.

That's a lesson that runs through [[@sports-science|sports science]]: sometimes the equipment changes first, and the technique follows.

{{story:gravity-fall}}

Uncoiled, the cochlea would be about 3.5 cm long. Different parts of it respond to different pitches. Hair cells near the start pick up high sounds, like a whistle. Those deep in the curl pick up low sounds, like a truck's rumble. It's a little like the keys of a [[@piano|piano]], laid out in order inside a shell the size of a pea.

Young people can hear from about 20 to 20,000 vibrations a second. As people age, the highest sounds usually fade first. Bats and dogs can hear much higher sounds than we can, and [[@echolocation|bats]] use them to find insects in the dark.

{{story:beethoven}}

By the 1960s, smallpox had disappeared from Europe and North America but was still common in South Asia, Africa and South America. In 1967, the WHO began an intensified campaign.

Vaccinating everyone was impossible, so teams used a clever plan. Whenever a case was found, they vaccinated everyone around that person, building a ring of protected people the virus couldn't cross. A simple forked needle made vaccinating quick and cheap.

{{story:united-nations}}

Art Fry worked in a different part of 3M. In 1974, he coated the edge of some paper with Silver's glue. The bookmarks stayed put, and lifted off without ripping the thin hymn book pages.

Then Fry realised something bigger. He wrote a question on one, stuck it on a report and passed it to his boss. His boss wrote the answer on the same note and stuck it back. That was the moment the idea changed from bookmark to message.

{{story:glue-sticky}}

Samuel Morse was an American painter who became obsessed with sending messages by electricity. After years of experiments and struggling for money, he persuaded the [[@congress-usa|US Congress]] to pay for a line between Washington, D.C. and Baltimore, about 60 km apart.

On 24 May 1844, Morse tapped out a message from the Capitol building in Washington: "What hath God wrought". His partner Alfred Vail received it in Baltimore and sent it straight back. Within a few years, telegraph wires on wooden poles stretched along railway lines across the United States and Europe.

{{story:codes-morse}}

The government set up so-called homelands for Black South Africans, often on poor land far from jobs. Millions of people were forced to move. Many men had to live in crowded hostels near mines and cities, seeing their families only once a year.

Schools for Black children got far less money. A 1953 law shaped their lessons to train them mainly for low-paid work. In 1976, students in Soweto, near Johannesburg, marched against a rule forcing them to learn in Afrikaans. Police opened fire, and many young people were killed. South Africa now marks 16 June as Youth Day.

{{story:cricket-history}}

Maya writing used hundreds of signs called glyphs, some standing for whole words and some for sounds. For a long time no one could read them. From the 1950s to the 1980s, scholars including Yuri Knorozov and Tatiana Proskouriakoff cracked much of the code.

The Maya counted in twenties and used a shell sign for [[@zero-invent|zero]]. They tracked the Sun, the Moon and [[@venus-hot|Venus]] carefully. Their Long Count calendar counted days from a starting point in 3114 BCE. One long cycle ended in December 2012, which led to a modern myth about the end of the world.

{{story:chocolate-making}}

Mahavira taught that every living thing has a soul, from humans and animals down to insects, plants and even tiny beings in water and earth. Harming any of them weighs down your own soul.

That is why some Jain monks and nuns sweep the path in front of them with a soft brush and cover their mouths with cloth. Many Jains eat before sunset, so they don't swallow insects in the dark. They are strict [[@vegetarian|vegetarians]], and many avoid root vegetables. It is a very demanding way to live, and Jains take it seriously.

{{story:vegetarian}}

In November they sighted Cape Cod, far north of where they had permission to settle. Some passengers grumbled that no rules would apply there. So before landing, 41 men signed an agreement promising to make and obey fair laws together.

This Mayflower Compact was a short document, only about 200 words. Women did not sign, and neither did most servants. But it was an early example of settlers agreeing to govern themselves, and Americans still point to it as a step towards self-government.

{{story:boats-sail}}

[[@antarctica|Antarctica]] gets so little snow inland that it is drier than much of the Sahara. Parts of it, called the Dry Valleys, have had no snow or ice cover for a very long time, and the ground is bare rock and gravel.

The Gobi Desert in Asia is cold too. Summers can be hot, but winters fall below minus 30 °C. Explorers in the 1920s found some of the first known dinosaur eggs in the Gobi, a story told in [[@dino-eggs|dinosaur eggs]]. [[@ladakh|Ladakh]], in India, is often called a cold desert, because it gets little rain and has freezing winters.

{{story:dino-eggs}}

Scripts have not always picked a side. The Phoenicians, who lived on the coast of today's Lebanon about 3,000 years ago, wrote right to left. The early Greeks borrowed their letters and at first wrote right to left too. Sometimes they went back and forth, turning at the end of each line like an ox ploughing a field. Later the Greeks settled on left to right, and Latin and English followed them.

Arabic, Hebrew and Persian kept the older direction. Chinese and Japanese were traditionally written top to bottom. [[@scripts-india|Indian scripts]] like Devanagari and Tamil run left to right.

{{story:zero-invent}}

From the 1890s to 1928, an Irish scholar named George Grierson led the first Linguistic Survey of India. His team collected samples of hundreds of languages and [[@dialects|dialects]], often by asking local people to translate the same short story, the Biblical parable of the Prodigal Son. Gramophone recordings made for the survey can still be heard today.

Every ten years, the [[@census|census]] asks each person their mother tongue. In 2011, Hindi came first, with about 53 crore speakers, followed by Bengali, Marathi, Telugu and Tamil. Many Indians speak two or three languages every day: one at home, one at school and one at the market or on the cricket field.

{{story:states-india}}

For most of history, nobody knew why milk turned to curd. In the 1670s Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a Dutch cloth trader, built small, powerful lenses and saw "little animals" swimming in drops of water. These were the first microbes anyone had seen.

{{story:microscopes}}

Two hundred years later, the French scientist Louis Pasteur showed that fermentation was caused by living microbes, not by a chemical reaction on its own. His work helped explain why wine went sour and why some foods spoiled.

The first time someone with an allergy meets the food, nothing much may happen. But the body makes special [[antibody|antibodies]] that remember the protein. These antibodies sit on immune cells like tripwires.

When the food arrives again, the protein trips the wire and the cells burst open, flooding the body with alarm chemicals. A reaction can begin within minutes. Even a tiny crumb can be enough for some people, which is why they must check every label.

{{story:immune-system}}

In the 1800s, women in San Antonio set up tables in the city's plazas each evening. They cooked pots of chili con carne, a stew of meat and dried [[@chillies|chillies]], over open fires and served it by lantern light. Visitors called them the "chili queens".

Travellers wrote home about them, and chili became famous across the United States. City health rules shut the stalls down in the 1930s and 1940s. By then, canned chili and chili powder were sold in grocery stores. In 1977, Texas made chili its official state dish.

